' The ONE definition of how a JellyRock dialog is laid out.
'
' Every dialog in the family — JRDialog, JRListDialog, QuickConnectDialog,
' OverviewDialog — is the same vertical flow with a different body, and after the
' fixed-panel and outside-footer modes were deleted it is the ONLY flow there is:
'
'   padding | title | TITLE_GAP | accent | BODY_GAP | [subheading | SUBHEADING_GAP |] body | BODY_GAP | footer | padding
'
' The accent rule sits TIGHT under the title (TITLE_GAP) because the two are one
' unit — it belongs to the title, it does not divide the card. Below it the body
' is centred in its own space (BODY_GAP above and below), so the eye reads
' title -> body -> options as three steps. Hierarchy comes from the accent, not
' from unequal gaps.
'
' The optional subheading is a lead line for the body, not a third step: it sits
' inside the body's space at SUBHEADING_GAP, so the pair still reads as one block.
'
' WHY THIS IS A MODULE AND NOT THREE COPIES. It was three copies. JRDialog was
' restyled during the #757 review — buttons inside the panel, a 3px border, a
' short accent rule instead of a full-width divider, a derived flow instead of
' fixed offsets — and JRListDialog and OverviewDialog kept the OLD look, because
' each carried its own private constants and its own layout code. Nothing caught
' it: no gate asserted a single position, gap, colour or asset, so "the app has
' one dialog language" stayed true only where someone had recently looked. These
' functions are pure so that claim is a test instead of an opinion.
'
' Pure on purpose, in the second sense too: no node access, no m, no global
' reads. Layout is the part that was impossible to check without a device, and
' it is the part that drifted.
import "pkg:/source/utils/misc.bs"

' Screen geometry. The app renders at 1920x1080 and Roku OS downscales to 720p.
const SCREEN_WIDTH = 1920
const SCREEN_HEIGHT = 1080

' The spacing scale, on a 6px grid. Two separate things are going on here and
' they were previously conflated:
'
'   The 6px RHYTHM is a design convention — it keeps the flow on one scale.
'   That is all it is. It does NOT, by itself, buy anything at 720p.
'
'   CRISPNESS at 720p comes from where an edge finally LANDS. Roku renders the
'   full 1920x1080 framebuffer and supersamples it (manifest, #419), so an edge
'   whose ABSOLUTE y is divisible by 3 sits on an output-row boundary and stays
'   sharp; one that is not spans three rows partially and goes soft.
'
' Divisible constants never delivered the second thing, because every offset is
' added to a panel origin derived from a MEASURED text height. Absolute edges
' landed on the grid about a third of the time — chance. snapToGrid() below is
' what actually delivers it; the spec asserts the edges, not the constants.
const PANEL_PADDING = 48
const TITLE_GAP = 24
const BODY_GAP = 36
const SEPARATOR_HEIGHT = 3

' Gap between an optional subheading and the body it leads. Deliberately TIGHTER
' than BODY_GAP and equal to TITLE_GAP, for the same reason: the subheading and
' the body are one unit. At BODY_GAP the two would read as separate blocks and
' the subheading would look like a second title.
const SUBHEADING_GAP = 24

' The 720p output row. 1080 / 720 = 3 source rows per 2 output rows, so an
' absolute coordinate divisible by 3 falls on an output-row boundary.
const PIXEL_GRID = 3

' Panel widths step in 6, not 3: centring HALVES the width, so only a multiple
' of 6 leaves the halved value on the 3px grid.
const PANEL_WIDTH_SCALE = 6

' Row geometry for the list dialog's options. Here rather than in the components
' so the spacing gate below covers them: they asserted the 6px rhythm in their
' own comments with nothing enforcing it, which is how a scale drifts.
'
' LIST_ROW_CHECK_GUTTER = LIST_ROW_PADDING + 36 icon slot + 24 gap. A fixed gutter on
' EVERY row, selected or not, so the labels line up with each other.
const LIST_ROW_HEIGHT = 60
const LIST_ROW_SPACING = 24
const LIST_ROW_PADDING = 24
const LIST_ROW_CHECK_GUTTER = 84

' Minimum width of a dialog footer button. Shared because THREE places need the
' same number — JRDialog sizes its row with it, choicesFitAsButtons estimates
' against it to decide button-row vs list, and OverviewDialog's OK uses it — and
' the last two previously carried their own copy of the literal.
'
' 204, not 200: on the 6px scale so the button's own edges land on the 720p grid.
const DIALOG_BUTTON_MIN_WIDTH = 204

' How a TextButton sizes itself: `visibleTextHeight + padding * 2`, where
' visibleTextHeight is `font.size * 0.7` and `padding` defaults to 30
' (components/ui/button/TextButton.xml). Mirrored here so the FALLBACK below is
' one definition rather than one per dialog.
const DIALOG_BUTTON_TEXT_RATIO = 0.7
const DIALOG_BUTTON_PADDING = 30

' A short accent rule under the title, not a full-width divider: at content width
' it competed with the body instead of supporting the title.
'
' Rendered in colorSecondary, NOT colorPrimary — colorPrimary is the theme's
' FOCUSABLE colour (it is the focus ring on the buttons in this same dialog) and
' this rule can never take focus. See components/CLAUDE.md's theme-colour table.
const SEPARATOR_ACCENT_WIDTH = 120

' Ceiling so a panel can never exceed the screen — at 1080 this leaves 78px top
' and bottom. A dialog that reaches it is holding content that belongs in
' showInfoDialog, so callers are expected to notice rather than silently clip.
'
' The 60 is the value #757 chose; consolidating the three copies into this
' module briefly changed it to 24, which loosened JRDialog's message ceiling by
' 36px. The spec pins it now, because the before/after render comparison that
' verified the consolidation could not see it — the ceiling only engages at
' roughly 600 characters of message, four times taller than anything captured.
const PANEL_MAX_HEIGHT = SCREEN_HEIGHT - (PANEL_PADDING * 2) - 60

' Compute every rect a dialog needs, from the heights it has measured.
'
' ONE SHAPE, for every dialog. The footer always flows INSIDE the panel and the
' panel is always derived from its content — there is no fixed-panel mode and no
' outside-footer mode, because a second way to lay out a dialog is how the app
' grew two dialog looks the first time. Both variants existed for OverviewDialog
' alone and both are gone: a before/after capture on device showed the outside
' button reading as detached over the backdrop with several hundred pixels of
' dead panel above it, and once the button is inside, the panel ceiling bounds
' the button too, which is what made content-sizing safe.
'
' @param {object} spec
'   panelWidth   - the panel's width; the caller owns width policy (JRDialog
'                  grows to fit its button row, the others are fixed)
'   titleHeight  - measured title height, 0 for a dialog with no title
'   subheadingHeight - measured height of an optional bold lead line above the
'                  body, 0 for none. Its slot only exists when it is non-zero,
'                  so a dialog without one is laid out exactly as before.
'   bodyHeight   - measured/derived body height, 0 for none. CLAMPED so the panel
'                  never exceeds PANEL_MAX_HEIGHT — read `body.height` back, do
'                  not assume you got what you asked for.
'   footerHeight - measured footer height, 0 for none
'
' @returns {object} - { panel, title, accent, subheading, body, footer }, each
'                     { x, y, width, height }, plus `overflows`.
'
' `overflows` means THE BODY DID NOT FIT and was clamped — what to do about it is
' the caller's, and the two answers differ: JRDialog truncates its message to
' `body.height` worth of lines (a confirm that scrolls is a confirm that should
' have been an info dialog), OverviewDialog scrolls its viewport at exactly that
' height. Both read the same field; neither re-derives the ceiling.
function computeDialogLayout(spec as object) as object
  panelWidth = intOr(spec.panelWidth, 0)
  titleHeight = intOr(spec.titleHeight, 0)
  subheadingHeight = intOr(spec.subheadingHeight, 0)
  bodyHeight = intOr(spec.bodyHeight, 0)
  footerHeight = intOr(spec.footerHeight, 0)

  ' Snap the MEASURED heights up to the grid before anything is added to them.
  ' A label's rendered height is whatever the font gave us, so without this one
  ' arbitrary value knocks every edge below it off the grid. Up, not down, so a
  ' block is never given less room than it measured; the cost is at most 2px.
  titleHeight = snapToGrid(titleHeight)
  subheadingHeight = snapToGrid(subheadingHeight)
  bodyHeight = snapToGrid(bodyHeight)
  footerHeight = snapToGrid(footerHeight)

  hasTitle = titleHeight > 0
  hasSubheading = subheadingHeight > 0
  contentWidth = panelWidth - (PANEL_PADDING * 2)

  ' --- flow, in panel-local coordinates -------------------------------------
  y = PANEL_PADDING
  titleY = y
  accentY = y
  if hasTitle
    y += titleHeight + TITLE_GAP
    accentY = y
    y += SEPARATOR_HEIGHT + BODY_GAP
  end if
  subheadingY = y
  if hasSubheading then y += subheadingHeight + SUBHEADING_GAP
  bodyY = y

  ' A gap only exists BETWEEN things. A dialog whose body is the last thing in
  ' the panel (JRListDialog has no footer at all) gets padding under it and
  ' nothing else — reserving a footer's gap for a footer that is not there left
  ' 84px of dead space under the list, which is what this shape is guarding.
  hasFooter = footerHeight > 0

  ' --- clamp the body so the panel can never exceed the ceiling --------------
  ' Everything below the body is a known quantity by now, so the tallest body
  ' that still fits is arithmetic rather than a second pass. Clamping HERE, in
  ' the one place that owns the flow, is why no caller has to re-derive the
  ' ceiling: a dialog asks for the body it measured and is told what it got.
  belowBody = PANEL_PADDING
  if hasFooter then belowBody += BODY_GAP + footerHeight
  maxBodyHeight = PANEL_MAX_HEIGHT - bodyY - belowBody
  if maxBodyHeight < 0 then maxBodyHeight = 0

  overflows = false
  if bodyHeight > maxBodyHeight
    bodyHeight = maxBodyHeight
    overflows = true
    ' Deliberately NOT snapped, unlike the measured heights above. This one is
    ' an ALLOWANCE bounded by the ceiling, and snapping it UP would defeat the
    ' clamp it was just subjected to.
  end if

  contentBottom = bodyY + bodyHeight

  ' The footer's slot is always BODY_GAP below the body; what a missing footer
  ' changes is only whether the panel reserves room for it. (`footer.height` is
  ' 0 in that case, so the rect is empty wherever it nominally sits.)
  footerY = contentBottom + BODY_GAP
  if hasFooter
    panelHeight = footerY + footerHeight + PANEL_PADDING
  else
    panelHeight = contentBottom + PANEL_PADDING
  end if

  ' The clamp above bounds the BODY, which is the whole story only while the body
  ' is what grew. A title tall enough to blow the ceiling on its own leaves
  ' maxBodyHeight at 0 and clamps nothing, so the panel can still come back over
  ' the line — say so rather than reporting a fit nobody checked.
  if panelHeight > PANEL_MAX_HEIGHT then overflows = true

  ' --- place the panel, then resolve everything to screen coordinates --------
  ' Both origins snap to the grid, because every offset is measured from them —
  ' they decide whether anything else lands on it. Centring halves a snapped
  ' height, which re-introduces a 1.5px offset, hence snapping the result too.
  ' Moves the panel by at most 2px, which is invisible; the spec pins the drift.
  '
  ' panelX only guarantees the LEFT edge. The right edge is panelX + panelWidth,
  ' so it additionally needs a width on the 6px scale — which every caller now
  ' uses, and which is why a width divisible by 6 lands panelX on the grid by
  ' itself ((1920 - w) / 2 = 960 - w/2). The snap is the floor for a future
  ' caller that picks a width off the scale: one crisp edge beats none.
  panelX = floorToGrid(int((SCREEN_WIDTH - panelWidth) / 2))
  panelY = floorToGrid(int((SCREEN_HEIGHT - panelHeight) / 2))

  layout = {
    panel: layoutRect(panelX, panelY, panelWidth, panelHeight),
    title: layoutRect(panelX + PANEL_PADDING, panelY + titleY, contentWidth, titleHeight),
    accent: layoutRect(panelX + int((panelWidth - SEPARATOR_ACCENT_WIDTH) / 2), panelY + accentY, SEPARATOR_ACCENT_WIDTH, SEPARATOR_HEIGHT),
    subheading: layoutRect(panelX + PANEL_PADDING, panelY + subheadingY, contentWidth, subheadingHeight),
    body: layoutRect(panelX + PANEL_PADDING, panelY + bodyY, contentWidth, bodyHeight),
    footer: layoutRect(panelX + PANEL_PADDING, panelY + footerY, contentWidth, footerHeight),
    hasTitle: hasTitle,
    hasSubheading: hasSubheading,
    overflows: overflows
  }

  return layout
end function

' Height a dialog's footer button (or button row) will have once it has sized
' itself, for the layout pass that runs BEFORE it has. `localBoundingRect()`
' reports 0 until then.
'
' A fallback rather than an early return at the call site, and all three callers
' need it for the same reason: a dialog with no title and no body is a legitimate
' state — it is the one every spec constructs — and returning would park it with
' an unlaid-out panel forever, since nothing else would fire to bring it back.
'
' One definition because there were three, verbatim, including the literal 60.
' They were free to drift from TextButton and from each other, and the whole
' point of this module is that geometry has one home.
'
' Takes the font size rather than reading `m.global.constants` itself: this
' module is pure on purpose (no node access, no m, no global reads), and that is
' what lets the spec above it run without a device.
function dialogButtonFallbackHeight(fontSize as integer) as integer
  return int(fontSize * DIALOG_BUTTON_TEXT_RATIO) + (DIALOG_BUTTON_PADDING * 2)
end function

' Centre a block of `width` inside a rect — for a footer button that is narrower
' than the content column it was allotted.
function centreInRect(container as object, width as integer) as integer
  return container.x + int((container.width - width) / 2)
end function

' ---- internals ----

' Round a measured height UP onto the output-row grid. 0 stays 0, so a block
' that is absent stays absent rather than becoming 3px tall.
function snapToGrid(value as integer) as integer
  if value <= 0 then return 0

  remainder = value mod PIXEL_GRID
  if remainder = 0 then return value
  return value + (PIXEL_GRID - remainder)
end function

' Round a panel width UP onto the 6px scale, so BOTH vertical edges land on the
' output grid: a width divisible by 6 makes the centred panelX divisible by 3
' ((1920 - w) / 2 = 960 - w/2), and the right edge is panelX + width.
'
' For a caller whose width GROWS to fit its content (JRDialog's button row), the
' fixed constant being on the scale is not enough — the grown value is derived
' from a measured row width and lands wherever it lands. Costs at most 5px of
' width, which no layout here is tight enough to notice.
function snapPanelWidthToScale(width as integer) as integer
  if width <= 0 then return 0

  remainder = width mod PANEL_WIDTH_SCALE
  if remainder = 0 then return width
  return width + (PANEL_WIDTH_SCALE - remainder)
end function

' Round DOWN onto the grid, for values that are an allowance rather than a
' measurement. Negatives clamp to 0: the only way to get one is a panel taller
' than the screen, which the caller is already told about via `overflows`.
function floorToGrid(value as integer) as integer
  if value <= 0 then return 0
  return value - (value mod PIXEL_GRID)
end function

' Named layoutRect, not rect: source/ files auto-scope into every component that
' imports them, and `rect` is a local variable name in half the layout code in
' this app (it collided with JRDialog's on the first build).
function layoutRect(x as integer, y as integer, width as integer, height as integer) as object
  return { x: x, y: y, width: width, height: height }
end function

' Missing and non-numeric both mean "not supplied". Callers pass measured values
' that are invalid until the node has rendered, and a layout computed from those
' is discarded and recomputed — it must not fault on the way through.
'
' `roInteger` is in this list because BrightScript reports integers under THREE
' names (roInt / roInteger / Integer) depending on how the value was boxed, and
' an allowlist missing one silently returns the fallback for every value. Every
' numeric type check in this repo carries all three for the same reason (see
' misc.bs); omitting it here made a whole layout compute from zeros and read as
' a dozen unrelated assertion failures.
function intOr(value as dynamic, fallback as integer) as integer
  if not isValid(value) then return fallback

  valueType = LCase(type(value))
  numericTypes = {
    "roint": 1, "rointeger": 1, "integer": 1,
    "rofloat": 1, "float": 1,
    "rodouble": 1, "double": 1,
    "rolonginteger": 1, "longinteger": 1
  }
  if numericTypes.doesExist(valueType) then return int(value)

  return fallback
end function
